Q: How to style a disabled radio button? A: <p>As Cuberic also pointed out in his answer, you can use an :after pseudo-element to Revlon the radio buttons. But to target just the <strong>checked</strong> radio buttons via CSS, use:</p>
<pre><code>input:disabled:checked:after{}
</code></pre>
<p>So, if you want to make the checked radio button stand out a little:</p>
<p>I used a <code>red</code> border in the example, but I personally prefer <code>#777777cc</code> as the border color for checked radio buttons.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>input:disabled{position:relative;}
input:disabled:checked:after{position:absolute;content:'';top:5%;width:80%;height:80%;border-radius:50%;border:1px solid red;} /* also try: #777777cc */</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;table&gt;&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
  &lt;td&gt;One:&lt;input type="radio" disabled checked&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;td&gt;Two:&lt;input type="radio" disabled&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;td&gt;333:&lt;input type="radio" disabled&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
  &lt;td&gt;One:&lt;input type="radio" disabled&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;td&gt;Two:&lt;input type="radio" disabled checked&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;td&gt;333:&lt;input type="radio" disabled&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
  &lt;td&gt;One:&lt;input type="radio" disabled&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;td&gt;Two:&lt;input type="radio" disabled&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;td&gt;333:&lt;input type="radio" disabled checked&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Note that you might need to tweak the <code>top</code>, <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> values a little bit for optimal display in your own containers.</p>

Q: Get the first element of an array A: <p>From PHP8.5, <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.array-first.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>array_first()</code></a> is available as a pointer-ignorant native function to access the first value in an array. <a href="https://3v4l.org/eIPon#vgit.master_jit" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$array = [
    4 =&gt; 'apple',
    7 =&gt; 'orange',
    13 =&gt; 'plum'
];

var_export(
    array_first($array)
);
// 'apple'
</code></pre>
<p>This new function was unanimously supported along with <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.array-last.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>array_last()</code></a>.</p>
<p>If the input array is empty, this function returns <code>null</code> (which might also be the value of a non-empty array).</p>
<p>Resources</p>
<ul>
<li><p><a href="https://php.watch/rfcs/array_first_last" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://php.watch/rfcs/array_first_last</a></p>
</li>
<li><p><a href="https://php.watch/versions/8.5/array_first-array_last" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://php.watch/versions/8.5/array_first-array_last</a> (which includes the following polyfill)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>The polyfill:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>function array_first(array $array): mixed {  
  return $array === [] ? null : $array[array_key_first($array)];  
}
</code></pre>

Q: PHP, $regex to obtain each URL of videos about $pattern A: <p>Use a DOM parser if you can, it will be safer than a regular expression.</p>
<p>But with a regular expression, I would suggest using the <strong>x</strong> flag for the
e<strong>x</strong>tended notation with spaces and comments and the <strong>i</strong> flag for
case-<strong>i</strong>nsensitive:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>/
\s                    # href should be preceded by a space.
href=&quot;(?&lt;href&gt;[^&quot;]+)&quot; # Capture the href value (⚠️ simple solution).
[^&gt;]*                 # Any other attribute after href.
&gt;                     # Closing of opening &lt;a&gt; tag.
(?&lt;link_text&gt;         # Capture the text of the link.
  [^&lt;]*?              # Anything before &quot;Greta&quot;.
  \bGreta\b           # The word &quot;Greta&quot;.
  [^&lt;]*               # Anything after &quot;Greta&quot;.
)
/gix
</code></pre>
<p>I'm capturing the <code>href</code> value and the link text in two named groups. This
can be done with the <code>(?&lt;group_name&gt; ... )</code> syntax.</p>
<p>Notice that <code>href=&quot;...&quot;</code> could also be <code>href = &quot;...&quot;</code> or <code>href='...'</code>, which
I don't take into consideration, to simplify my answer. But if you want to
be sure it works, <strong>you should use a DOM parser</strong>. If not, you end up with a
regular expression getting long and complicated,
like this: <a href="https://regex101.com/r/8VKVxO/1" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://regex101.com/r/8VKVxO/1</a></p>
<p>But it's the only way to handle HTML, which can be in multiple forms...</p>
<p>You may have some other attributes after the <code>href</code> attribute, so take it
in consideration with <code>[^&gt;]*</code> to match any char not being the closing tag.</p>
<p>And for &quot;Greta&quot; itself, use the word boundary <code>\b</code> before and after, if
not you could match something like &quot;gretard&quot;, which wouldn't be what you
are looking for.</p>
<p>You can see the result here: <a href="https://regex101.com/r/9wYKYK/1" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://regex101.com/r/9wYKYK/1</a></p>
<p>In PHP:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?php

$data = &lt;&lt;&lt;DATA
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;Greta Thunberg talk about ...&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;another video of Greta Thunberg&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qJv-upsvMfM&quot;&gt;Israel and Greta Thunberg&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another video&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another video&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;...'
DATA;

$regex = &lt;&lt;&lt;REGEX
/
href=&quot;(?&lt;href&gt;[^&quot;]+)&quot; # Capture the href value.
[^&gt;]*                 # Any other attribute after href.
&gt;                     # Closing of opening &lt;a&gt; tag.
(?&lt;link_text&gt;         # Capture the text of the link.
  [^&lt;]*?              # Anything before &quot;Greta&quot;.
  \bGreta\b           # The word &quot;Greta&quot;.
  [^&lt;]*               # Anything after &quot;Greta&quot;.
)
/ix
REGEX;

preg_match_all($regex, $data, $matches, PREG_SET_ORDER);

print_r($matches);
</code></pre>
<p>Run it here: <a href="https://onlinephp.io/c/87b952" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://onlinephp.io/c/87b952</a></p>
<h2>The DOM parser solution, which is safer</h2>
<p>In PHP, we can use <em>DOMDocument</em> and the <em>XPath</em> query feature, to find
all the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tags containing a specific text within some rather crappy
but valid HTML code (attributes with double or simple quotes, or none,
and spaces around the &quot;=&quot; sign) :</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?php

$data = &lt;&lt;&lt;DATA
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;Greta Thunberg talk about...&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href = 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok'&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href ='https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok'&gt;a video of greta thunberg&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href='https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok'&gt;Not Gretard Thunberg&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&gt;Greta is fine&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href =  &quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qJv-upsvMfM&quot;&gt;Israel and Greta&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;Neither Hogreta&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another video&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
...
DATA;

$dom = new DOMDocument();
// Suppress warnings for malformed HTML
libxml_use_internal_errors(true);
$dom-&gt;loadHTML($data);
libxml_clear_errors();

$xpath = new DOMXPath($dom);

// The word to search, in lowercase.
$word_to_search = 'greta';
// The same word to search, in uppercase. Used in the translate() function of
// the XPath query, later below.
$word_to_search_uppercase = strtoupper($word_to_search);
// A regular expression, used as security, to match &quot;greta&quot; as a word only.
// Positive lookbehind for begin of string or a space char.
// Positive lookahead for end of string or a space char.
$regex_word_to_search = '/(?&lt;=^|\s)' . preg_quote($word_to_search) . '(?=\s|$)/i';

// Let's use a XPath query to get all &lt;a&gt; tags with a text containing
// the word to search in lowercase, after having transformed all
// uppercase letters of this word into the corresponding lowercase letter.
// In XPath 2.0, you could use the lower-case() function instead of this
// use of the translate() function.
$query = &lt;&lt;&lt;XPATH_QUERY
//a[contains(
    translate(text(), '$word_to_search_uppercase', '$word_to_search'), 
    '$word_to_search'
)]
XPATH_QUERY;

echo &quot;XPath query:\n$query\n\n&quot;;

$links = $xpath-&gt;query($query);
$urls = [];

echo &quot;XPath results:\n&quot;;
foreach ($links as $link) {
    // Display the found link and href value.
    echo &quot;Found: &quot; . $link-&gt;nodeValue . &quot;\thref: &quot; . $link-&gt;getAttribute('href');
    // Check that it's really a word and not part of a word.
    if (preg_match($regex_word_to_search, $link-&gt;nodeValue)) {
        echo &quot;\tOK\n&quot;;
        // Store the URL and link title in a dictionnary, in case we have
        // several times the same href value within different links.
        $urls[$link-&gt;getAttribute('href')][] = $link-&gt;nodeValue;
    } else {
        echo &quot;\tNot a word!\n&quot;;
    }
}
echo &quot;\n&quot;;

echo &quot;\$urls = &quot; . var_export($urls, true) . &quot;\n&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>Run it live here: <a href="https://onlinephp.io/c/5302e" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://onlinephp.io/c/5302e</a></p>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>XPath query:
//a[contains(
    translate(text(), 'GRETA', 'greta'), 
    'greta'
)]

XPath results:
Found: Greta Thunberg talk about... href: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok   OK
Found: a video of greta thunberg    href: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok   OK
Found: Not Gretard Thunberg href: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok   Not a word!
Found: Greta is fine    href: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok   OK
Found: Israel and Greta href: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qJv-upsvMfM   OK
Found: Neither Hogreta  href: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok   Not a word!

$urls = array (
  'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok' =&gt; 
  array (
    0 =&gt; 'Greta Thunberg talk about...',
    1 =&gt; 'a video of greta thunberg',
  ),
  'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok' =&gt; 
  array (
    0 =&gt; 'Greta is fine',
  ),
  'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qJv-upsvMfM' =&gt; 
  array (
    0 =&gt; 'Israel and Greta',
  ),
)
</code></pre>
